      "body": "## Overview\n\nRuns each PyTorch Compilation root test file (`tests/compile/test_*.py`) in its own plain `pytest` command instead of the `find | xargs` loop, and enrolls the step and its MI300 mirror in `automatic_shard`. The `find | xargs` pipe isn't plain pytest, so an enrollment flag alone would be ignored.\n\n## Claims\n\n- The same 13 root files run, each in its own process, as today. Subdirectories still run in their own steps.\n- The step's commands are now plain pytest, so the planner can size it. Both the step and its mirror plan one job today and split by themselves once their tests grow.\n\n## Validation\n\n- A first version ran all root files in one pytest. Build 99664 failed 12 tests in `test_config.py` with `Cannot re-initialize CUDA in forked subprocess`: those tests use `@pytest.mark.forked`, and an earlier file had already initialized CUDA in the shared process. One process per file avoids this, as the old loop did.\n- The sharding harness and the generator's own enrollment gate accept the step and its mirror.\n- `split_check.py` finds no step whose jobs each run the whole suite.\n- `/ci run` build 99690 on this head passed. H200: 2 shards, slowest 12 minutes, 172 passed and 2 skipped across the 13 commands, the same totals as main's single command in build 99608; machine time about 10% over main. MI300: one job, 170 passed and 4 skipped, as on main.\n\n## Details\n\n- **New root files** need a line in the YAML; the comment above the commands says so.\n- **Timeouts unchanged.** `timeout_in_minutes` stays per job.\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [ ] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [ ] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [ ] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [ ] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n",
